    {
     "tabId": "1b35b644-78a0-467f-bc49-7882731b6b89",
     "collapsed": false,
     "id": "67e7d834-3f77-41a2-a9d2-48a17f14f453",
     "gridsterCoords": {
      "w": 12,
      "x": 1,
      "h": 7,
      "y": 22
     },
     "state": "",
     "type": "TextDisplay",
     "title": "How to read this",
     "config": {
      "locationUrl": "",
      "locationFile": "",
      "editorData": "<div style=\"font-family:'Metropolis','Segoe UI',sans-serif;font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;padding:6px 12px\">\n<h2 style=\"font-size:15px;color:#ffffff;margin:4px 0 10px 0\">DOM &mdash; client, owner, component manager</h2>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\">Storage is slow cluster-wide and the answer is nearly always one of DOM's three roles. They are stacked here so the comparison is immediate.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">The three roles</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\"><b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Client</b> is where a VM's IO enters vSAN &mdash; what the guest feels. <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Owner</b> coordinates the write across replicas and enforces the storage policy. <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Component Manager</b> is what the local disks actually did.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Reading it</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\"><b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Client high, Comp Mgr low</b> &mdash; the problem is above the disks: network, owner coordination, or policy work.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\"><b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Both high</b> &mdash; the disks. Go to the storage screen and look at DAVG.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\"><b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Comp Mgr high alone</b> &mdash; a local device, or rebuild traffic competing with guest IO.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Congestion</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Congestions</span> is vSAN throttling itself on purpose when a layer cannot keep up. <span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Read / Write / Unmap Congestion</span> split it by operation. Unlike a latency figure there is no \"normal\" level &mdash; <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">any sustained non-zero value means the cluster is applying back-pressure</b>.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Per host</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\">The same client metrics broken out by host. Use it to tell <i style=\"color:#cfd8de\">the cluster is slow</i> from <i style=\"color:#cfd8de\">one host is slow and dragging the average</i>.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:14px 0 5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#b6c2ca;font-style:italic\">DOM Owner carries 288 per-host metrics and 46 at cluster scope &mdash; far too many for a rapid screen, so only the roll-up is shown here.</p>\n</div>\n",
